  "answer_text": "## What the verifier is telling you\n\nThe last meaningful log line is:\n\n```\nliveness analysis exceeded complexity limit (10001 calls)\nprocessed 0 insns ...\n```\n\nThis is not a \"your program is wrong\" error \u2014 it's a *resource/complexity* rejection. The program builds a call graph that is both **maximally deep and very wide**:\n\n- `entry \u2192 helper_a \u2192 helper_b \u2192 \u2026 \u2192 helper_g` is 8 frames deep\n  (that is exactly `MAX_CALL_FRAMES`, the deepest legal BPF call chain), and\n- every level issues **50** calls to the next level (`CALLS_50`).\n\nThe verifier's static liveness pass walks this call graph recursively. Because nothing is shared across the different call sites, the number of `(caller-frame, call-site)` analysis steps grows roughly like the number of root-to-leaf paths:\n\n```\nentry: 1\na:     50\nb:     50^2\n...\ng:     50^7  \u2248 7.8e11\n```\n\nThe pass gives up as soon as it has performed more than 10000 of these descents \u2014 which is why you see `subprog#7 helper_g ... analyzing (depth 7)` printed over and over and then the abort. The program is simply too large/branchy for the liveness analyzer; it can never be accepted at this size.\n\nSo there are only two ways to \"fix\" this:\n\n## Option A (most likely the intended one): expect the rejection\n\nThis kind of selftest \u2014 `__naked`, `SEC(\"?raw_tp\")`, macro-generated to deliberately push the verifier to a hard limit \u2014 is a *negative* test. It exists to prove the verifier bails out gracefully instead of looping/OOMing. The repair is to tell the test loader to expect that failure:\n\n```c\nSEC(\"?raw_tp\")\n__failure __msg(\"liveness analysis exceeded complexity limit\")\n__naked int entry(void)\n{\n\tasm volatile (\n\t\tCALLS_50(helper_a)\n\t\t\"r0 = 0;\"\n\t\t\"exit;\"\n\t\t::: __clobber_all);\n}\n```\n\nWith the `__failure`/`__msg` annotation, the selftest \"passes\" precisely because the verifier rejects the program with that message. This is the smallest source change and matches the program's evident purpose (it is engineered to overflow the limit and cannot be made loadable at this scale).\n\n## Option B: make the program actually load\n\nIf the goal really is for the verifier to accept it, you must shrink the call graph so the liveness traversal stays under ~10000 descents. With an 8-deep chain the per-level fan-out must drop to \u2264 3 (e.g. `3^7 \u2248 2187`, total \u2248 3280 < 10000). For example, redefine the fan-out macro:\n\n```c\n/* fan-out of 3 instead of 50 keeps the 8-deep chain under the limit */\n#define CALLS_50(fn) CALL_AT(fn, 8) CALL_AT(fn, 16) CALL_AT(fn, 24)\n```\n\n(or, equivalently, reduce the nesting depth). Note that simply switching `CALLS_50` to `CALLS_10` is *not* enough: `10^7` still blows the limit \u2014 you need fan-out around 3 at this depth.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nGiven the design (deep+wide macro expansion whose only possible outcome is hitting the liveness limit), Option A is the correct, minimal fix: add\n\n```c\n__failure __msg(\"liveness analysis exceeded complexity limit\")\n```\n\nto the `entry` program so the test expects the rejection. Use Option B only if the test was meant to demonstrate a *loadable* deep-call-chain.",
